      {
        "key": "li:7518551822157176832",
        "platform": "li",
        "label": "infographics-insights",
        "author_name": "Useful Infographics",
        "text": "Coaching and mentoring are not the same.\nThis is how they are different.\nAnd this is how to become a unique value-added coach.\n\n\n\nGreat post by: Jeroen Kraaijenbrink\n\n\nOriginal post below\n\n\n⬇️⬇️⬇️\n\n\nCoaching and mentoring are not the same.\nThis is how they are different.\nAnd this is how to become a unique value-added coach.\n\nMany people speak about coaching and mentoring as if they are interchangeable. They are not. And understanding the difference is one of the most important things you can do for your own development and for the growth of the people you support.\n\nThis visual captures a truth that often gets lost in practice. Coaching and mentoring sit on the same spectrum, yet they serve different purposes. \n\nCoaching creates space for people to think for themselves. It invites reflection, draws out insight, and helps someone strengthen their own clarity and autonomy. The coach does not tell you what to do. The coach makes it possible for you to discover it.\n\nMentoring works differently. It draws on the lived experience of someone who has walked the path before you. The mentor shares stories, offers guidance, transfers knowledge and culture, and helps you navigate decisions with wisdom earned over time. A great mentor can shorten your learning curve in ways no book or course ever can.\n\nBoth practices share a deep human foundation: empathy, trust, curiosity, listening, and meaning making. These are the qualities that allow any form of guidance to become transformational instead of transactional.\n\nThe question is not which one is better. It is where you need to be on the spectrum at a specific moment. Sometimes you need a coach who helps you step back from the noise and find your own direction. Sometimes you need a mentor who helps you see around corners you have not yet learned to notice. And often, the most impactful relationships blend both, shifting seamlessly as the situation requires.\n\nThis is also why becoming a truly effective coach today requires more than a single skillset. You need the coaching foundations that cultivate autonomy and critical thinking. You also need the ability to draw from your experience responsibly and to know when guidance supports growth and when it risks taking it away.\n\n______________\n\n\nDM us to be featured on our next post\n\nFollow for more Useful Infographics",
        "created_at": "2026-10-21T06:00:42.340000+00:00",
        "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/infographics-insights_coaching-and-mentoring-are-not-the-same-activity-7518551822157176832-1Go6"
      },
